  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nCurrent visible satellite shows high clouds streaming in from the \nnorth while low clouds 30 miles off the coastline continue to \nscatter out. The thermal ridge axis remains over SoCal today, but \nhigh clouds will inhibit some of the afternoon peak heating. As \nsuch, today will mimic Monday with highs generally in the low to mid \n70s for the coast and valleys, 50s for the mountains, 60s for the \nHigh Desert and low 80s for the low desert. The ridge axis slides \neastward quickly today as a deepening low off the coast advances \ntowards Northern California. As this system moves inland this \nevening and the gradient tightens, gusty onshore winds will develop \nover the coastal waters, with a Small Craft Advisory currently in \nplace. Additional details can be found in the Marine Discussion \nsection below. \n\nTonight as this low moves in, an deepening marine layer across much \nof San Diego County will support some localized light rainfall early \nWednesday morning for far southern SD County. While chances continue \nto be 15% or less for any given area, a lucky few may experience \nsome wetting rain before sunrise. Otherwise, the general pattern \nthis week remains dry. \n\nThe low will move into Nevada on Wednesday, providing one final day \nof below-normal temperatures before a warming trend returns for late \nweek. With the departure of the low, subsidence builds over CA with \ntemperatures quickly warming to well-above-normal by Friday. \nGlobal models continue to set up high pressure over the Four \nCorners with a substantial ridge stretching from Canada down to \nMexico, expected to usher in offshore flow for the majority of \nCalifornia. While the exact set up remains in flux, especially on \nSaturday, by Sunday, alignment is better on weak to moderate Santa\nAnas bringing exceptionally warm temperatures and gusty winds to \ninland valleys and coastal regions. For now, have maintained Santa\nAna winds for both Saturday and Sunday, with highs for coastal \ncities in the upper 80s, quite a bit warmer than even some of the \ndeserts, and minor to Moderate HeatRisk will return for areas west\nof the mountains. Should the system pan out with a robust high \nover the Four Corners, easterly wind gusts could reach or exceed \n30-40 mph in some of the more wind-prone spots, especially the \nInland Empire and coastal Orange County. With these winds, \nhumidity may plummet both days to the teens, even for areas west \nof the mountains. We are still a bit far out to be nailing out the\ndetails, but prepare for a windy, dry, and abnormally hot \nweekend.",
